#### Verified comparator and individual comparison

The complete historical verification in `../freeze/OT_1999CHUNK5_COMPARATOR_VERIFIED.md` records April 17, 2000, No. 98-9349, 529 U.S. 334: reversal, 7–2. Rehnquist wrote for Stevens, O'Connor, Kennedy, Souter, Thomas and Ginsburg; Breyer dissented with Scalia. No partial join or additional writing is reported. Rehnquist supplies no simulated vote and historical authorship is not an assignment. The [Court opinion](https://www.law.cornell.edu/supct/html/98-9349.ZO.html) and [dissent](https://www.law.cornell.edu/supct/html/98-9349.ZD.html), including footnotes, were fully verified by the comparator source review.

| Justice | Historical alignment | Reconciled alignment and premise test |
|---|---|---|
| Stevens | Reverse; full search-rule join | Retain reversal. His Dickerson authorized-touch and actual-manipulation joins and independent luggage rule support the distinction between ordinary handling and examination of concealed contents. No changed premise warrants departure. |
| O'Connor | Reverse; full join | Retain reversal on the physical character of the examination. Her Dickerson and luggage positions fit the rule; Robinette does not revive a consent ground the Government does not rely on. |
| Scalia | Dissent with Breyer | Retain affirmance. The record's limited-contact finding and sizeable hard contents support his foreseeable-exposure application. His existing luggage and Dickerson joins protect privacy without deciding whether this particular information remained concealed from ordinary contact. |
| Kennedy | Reverse; full join | Retain reversal. His prior authorized-touch, separate-luggage and objective-consent positions support the claimed handling/examination boundary without an all-touch prohibition. |
| Souter | Reverse; full join | Retain reversal. The closed bag's actual exposure, not all conceivable unwanted handling, controls. His Dickerson and luggage joins support that distinction. |
| Thomas | Reverse; full join | Retain reversal. His nonjoin in the simulated Dickerson manipulation application does not reject its unanimous operative restriction. Here no initial protective authority exists and the shared facts establish an affirmative contents examination; no contradictory historical premise remains. |
| Ginsburg | Reverse; full join | Retain reversal. Her independent-luggage and objective-sequence positions preserve concealed effects despite lawful bus access. Later discoveries do not justify the earlier intrusion. |
| Breyer | Dissent | Retain affirmance. His practical exposure inquiry accepts closed-luggage privacy but regards this contact as within foreseeable compression and handling of the particular soft bag. That is consistent with his objective-review commitments and with the historical dissent. |

All eight historical alignments are retained on independently supported grounds; no historical departure is identified. The six support the same objective search rule and application, while Scalia and Breyer share the contrary exposure application. The latter two need no separate legal rule authorizing suspicionless luggage searches generally. A shared dissent can state their legal disagreement; assignment of its author is not resolved here.

#### Scope and remedy check

The actual examination exceeds ordinary handling under the six-Associate rule; private investigative purpose is not the trigger. Katz and Place protect the closed effects interest, while Hicks and the operative Dickerson rule distinguish authorized perception from added physical acquisition of concealed information. Ciraolo and Riley do not make every tactile method equivalent to visual observation. The opposing two contest the objective information boundary on these facts, not the existence of every privacy interest in luggage.

The interior checkpoint, ownership, zipped bag, overhead location, ordinary passenger access, disputed pants packaging and limited-contact characterization are already in the frozen neutral record. No exact squeeze count, closed-bin finding or new factual development is introduced. The Government's consent nonreliance was sanitized before modeling and is independently confirmed by the historical Court's footnote; it is not an excluded historical rationale newly supplying a model premise.

The six support reversal of the no-search ruling and a bounded remand in the actual criminal case. Bond's suppression motion concerns drugs and subsequent confessions. The handoff neither reduces the motion to the drugs nor decides every causal question concerning later warned statements. The Government receives no invented consent hearing or revived admission defense. No preserved independent justification is newly found, and no automatic acquittal, release, new trial or all-statements suppression rule follows. The two would affirm on absence of a search without reaching fruits of illegality.

